---
import Ecosystem from "../../components/Ecosystem.astro";
import SnippetBlock from "../../components/SnippetBlock.astro";
import Sheet from "../../layouts/Sheet.astro";
import { projectBriefRows } from "../../data/sharedtree-project-brief";

const source =
  "https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed/tree/main/examples/shared_tree_project_brief_lustre";
---

<Sheet
  title="watershed — Compare live SharedTree project briefs"
  description="Two people, one nested brief. Compare locally authored Fluid and watershed editors: object identity, live field edits, UUID-keyed milestones, and selection that isn't a lock."
>
  <header class="comparison-hero">
    <div>
      <p class="back-link"><a href="/sharedtree">Back to the SharedTree comparison</a></p>
      <h1>Two people.<br /><em>One living brief.</em></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Change the owner while someone else moves the target date. Add a
        milestone without taking their input away. These two runnable editors
        put the same nested form on native SharedTree—one in Fluid and React,
        one in watershed and Lustre. Here's where their APIs meet, and where
        they don't.
      </p>
      <div class="source-links">
        <a href={source}>Run the Gleam editor</a>
        <a href={`${source}/fluid`}>Run the Fluid editor</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="doc-body">
    <div class="g-block introduction">
      <h2>More than a shared array.</h2>
      <p>
        A title, an owner, a schedule, an optional review. Milestones sit inside
        the schedule's only array. The interesting part isn't the form—it's
        keeping an edit attached to the right object while another person
        changes what's around it. Start with the{" "}
        <a href="/sharedtree/item-counter">item-counter comparison</a>{" "}
        if you'd rather trace one array first.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both columns are <strong>locally authored example code in this repository</strong>.
        The Fluid app is not an official Fluid example or an upstream snapshot.
        It uses Fluid 3.1.0 and the <strong>experimental Signaler</strong> helper
        for field selection. Both apps run independently, against separate
        local services; neither opens the other's documents.
      </p>
      <nav aria-label="Comparison sections">
        {projectBriefRows.map(row => <a href={`#${row.id}`}>{
          { schema: "Schema", lifecycle: "Create and open", observe: "Read and subscribe",
            edits: "Property edits", milestones: "Milestone identity", presence: "Field selection" }[row.id]
        }</a>)}
      </nav>
    </div>

    {projectBriefRows.map(row => (
      <section class="g-block comparison-row" id={row.id} data-comparison-row={row.id} aria-labelledby={`${row.id}-title`}>
        <h2 id={`${row.id}-title`}>{row.title}</h2>
        <p>{row.explanation}</p>
        <div class="comparison-pair">
          <div class="comparison-column" data-comparison-column="fluid">
            <h3>Fluid · TypeScript</h3>
            {row.fluid.map(snippet => <SnippetBlock snippet={snippet} />)}
          </div>
          <div class="comparison-column" data-comparison-column="watershed">
            <h3>watershed · Gleam</h3>
            {row.watershed.map(snippet => <SnippetBlock snippet={snippet} />)}
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    ))}

    <section class="g-block run-guide" aria-labelledby="run-title">
      <h2>Try two fields at once.</h2>
      <p>
        From the repository root, start Floodgate on port 4000 with{" "}
        <code>just integration-up</code>. In{" "}
        <code>examples/shared_tree_project_brief_lustre</code>, run{" "}
        <code>pnpm install</code>, <code>pnpm build</code>, then{" "}
        <code>pnpm serve</code>. Open <code>http://127.0.0.1:8080</code>.
        Wait for the URL's <code>document</code> parameter, then copy the full
        URL into a second tab. Add <code>&amp;name=Sam</code> to give it another name.
      </p>
      <p>
        For Fluid, work in the example's <code>fluid/</code> directory:
        run <code>pnpm install</code> and <code>pnpm build</code>, then keep{" "}
        <code>pnpm service</code> running on port 7070 in one terminal and{" "}
        <code>pnpm serve</code> in another. Open{" "}
        <code>http://127.0.0.1:8081</code> and share that app's URL with another
        tab. The static host doesn't start the service. These are two separate
        runs, not two clients opening a cross-SDK document.
      </p>
      <p>
        In either app, focus <code>owner.name</code> in one tab and{" "}
        <code>schedule.target</code> in the other. Type without blurring:
        each remote value should appear while the fields are still focused.
        There's no Apply or Save step. Now select the same field in both tabs.
        Both names can appear; both people can type. The final resolved string
        agrees across clients, but simultaneous whole-string assignments can
        overwrite one another. This isn't collaborative character-level text.
      </p>
      <p>
        Keep a caret inside a milestone title and remove a preceding milestone
        from the other tab. The caret and selection badge belong to the UUID,
        not the old row number. Add and remove review remotely, too. A badge
        on a removed review or milestone must disappear rather than jump to a
        replacement.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="g-block limits" aria-labelledby="limits-title">
      <h2>The boundary is part of the example.</h2>
      <p>
        Dates and email stay ordinary strings so intermediate typing remains
        valid. Only an input in IME composition defers remote value writes;
        other fields keep updating. Completed composition publishes once,
        and native caret ranges are clamped when a remote string gets shorter.
        Disconnect disables edits and clears visible peers. Close stops
        awareness and disposes resources—it doesn't confirm delivery of pending
        edits. To check persistence, wait until the other tab receives an edit,
        then reload. Content returns; old selection doesn't.
      </p>
      <p>
        These apps mint development tokens in the browser. Don't deploy that
        authentication flow or put production secrets in browser code; issue
        production tokens on a backend. An uncertain creation isn't retried
        automatically. See the <a href={source}>README and complete sources</a>{" "}
        for setup, error handling, cleanup, and the real two-client browser gate.
        This is a comparison of these APIs, not a claim of full Fluid SDK parity.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Ecosystem />
</Sheet>

<style>
  .comparison-hero {
    padding: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 6rem) var(--content-pad);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }
  .comparison-hero > div { max-width: 72rem; margin-inline: auto; }
  .back-link { margin-bottom: var(--space-xl); font-size: var(--text-sm); }
  h1 {
    max-width: 20ch;
    font-size: var(--text-display);
    font-weight: 640;
    font-stretch: 122%;
    line-height: 0.99;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
  }
  h1 em { color: var(--overprint-deep); font-style: normal; }
  .lede {
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
    max-width: 60ch;
    font-size: clamp(1.125rem, 0.6vw + 1rem, 1.3125rem);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  a { color: var(--waterline); text-underline-offset: 0.15em; }
  a:hover { color: var(--overprint-deep); }
  .source-links, nav {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-md) var(--space-xl);
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
  }
  .doc-body { max-width: 76rem; }
  .run-guide code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .comparison-row {
    padding-top: var(--space-xl);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    scroll-margin-top: var(--space-xl);
  }
  .comparison-pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-xl);
  }
  .comparison-column { min-width: 0; }
  .comparison-column h3 {
    padding-bottom: var(--space-sm);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
  .comparison-column :global(.g-file) {
    display: block;
    width: fit-content;
    max-width: 100%;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .comparison-column :global(.g-code pre:focus-visible) {
    outline: 2px solid var(--overprint);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }
  .limits { margin-bottom: var(--space-2xl); }
  @media (max-width: 60rem) {
    .comparison-pair { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--space-lg); }
  }
  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 8.5vw, 2.7rem); font-stretch: 100%; }
  }
</style>
